# Report requirements

Soleox has three report sets: Owner Reports, Agent Reports, and Office Reports. Requirements in All Reports apply to every set.

When a new chat should follow this before editing reports, say: read `.cursor/rules/report-requirements.mdc` before making changes.

## All Reports

### Navigation

On a report page the top navigation is hidden. Hovering the top of the page slides it into view. It hides again when the pointer leaves it.

On phones and narrow screens (below `md`, 768px), the top navigation is a normal header at the top of the page that scrolls away with it; it does not hide or listen for the pointer. Its menu button opens the phone navigation sheet.

Below `md`, the phone menu sheet has a second level for a report section. Opened on an Owner Reports page, it starts on the Owner Reports level: an "Exit Owner Reports" row at the top returns to the first level, then the Owner Reports cover link and every report, numbered and grouped like the desktop reports menu, with the current report highlighted. On the first level, Owner Reports opens that second level. The numbered reports menu button beside the report title is hidden below `md`, so the title takes the full row. Later report sections (Agent Reports, Office Reports) follow the same pattern.

On every device a horizontal swipe pages between reports in menu order: a finger on touch screens, or a two-finger sideways swipe on a trackpad. Swipe left for the next report, right for the previous one. While swiping, the report stays still and a round arrow slides in from the edge the next report comes from, growing with the swipe. Once releasing will page, the arrow turns emerald and a pill beside it names that report. Releasing then fades the report out and the new one fades in with a short nudge from that side; a shorter swipe lets the arrow slide back out. There is no arrow past the first or last report. Arrow-key paging uses the same fade-in. A swipe that starts on something that scrolls sideways (a wide table), in a form field, within 24px of a touch screen's left or right edge, or while a menu or dropdown is open does nothing, so those keep their own scrolling and the iOS back gesture. Shift + mouse wheel scrolls sideways as usual and does not page. With reduced motion turned on, paging happens without the slide.

### Scrolling on phones

Below `md`, every report scrolls the page natively by touch. Agent Earnings, Team Caps, and Owner Summary with hidden scorecards or an open drilldown do not lock `html` and `body` to `overflow: hidden` or forward wheel scrolling there; that full-screen layout applies only at `md` and wider.

### Filters

Group, Status, Side, Primary agent, and Lead source use the multi-select control on every report. Metric stays a single-select: one measure. The closed control of every filter in the filters card is an underline field, the same field Cap year uses: the label sits above the value, the value sits on a bottom border, and a chevron sits at the right.

Date range is a single-select dropdown in that same underline field.

Below `md` the filters card is a fixed two-column grid; Date range spans both columns. Checkbox filters (Exclude Team Leads, Cumulative, Show all paid transactions) each take a full row, and their labels match the 14px filter values. Each filter fills its column and truncates long text with an ellipsis, so changing a selection never moves or resizes a filter. Date range stays on one line and truncates its dates.

On every device the filters card can collapse to one line to give the report more room. An up arrow in its top-right corner collapses it. The collapsed line reads Filters, the date range (or the cap year), and how many other filters are set, such as "2 filters"; tapping or clicking it opens the card again. The choice is remembered in that browser.

A dropdown filter does not accept typed text. The closed control is a button, and a choice is made from the open list. From and To are the only fields in the filters card that accept typed text.

From and To sit immediately to the right of the date range filter. No other filter comes between the date range and those two fields.

- From and To appear only when Custom is selected. Each is typed as MM/DD/YYYY, the year stops at four digits, and a calendar button opens the date picker.
- Every other option shows its name and, in grey, the dates that name covers, as `MM/DD/YYYY - MM/DD/YYYY`. Example: Year to date 01/01/2026 - 10/09/2026.
- Show a search box when there are more than 8 presets.

- When 1 to 3 options are selected, the closed filter lists those names. When 4 or more are selected, it shows the count and filter name, such as "4 groups" or "4 lead sources".
- When every option is unchecked, the filter shows None and highlights in amber so the empty selection is visible.
- The default is every listed option. A report may pre-select a subset instead. Leaderboard by Date, Team Performance, and Agent Earnings status default to Closed and Pending, and those reports never include any other status.
- Show a search box when there are more than 8 options.
- Each option shows a right-aligned record count: transactions with that value after the other filters, not after this filter.
- Hovering an option shows ONLY over its count. ONLY keeps that value and clears the others.
- On touch screens, tapping an option's checkbox or name checks or unchecks it. Tapping the rest of the row (the blank space or the count) shows ONLY over the count, and tapping ONLY applies it. Below `md` an open filter list stays within the screen width.
- Reset restores that filter’s default for the current report.
- Select all checks every option currently listed.
- Options stay dynamic. A value is listed only when the current report view still has records for it after the other filters.
- The open list is taller when the screen has room, up to 36rem, and it stays within the viewport when the screen is shorter.
- An open list's header (Select all, Reset, Search) stays flush with the list's top border while the options scroll, so no option shows above it.
- While any report dropdown is open (filters, the title metric and cap pickers, and the production period picker), wheel and touch scrolling move only that list. The report behind it does not scroll, on desktop and on phones.

### Tables

- A table header stays fixed just below the report title and filters while that table is on screen. The stuck header stays square so the table body does not show past its corners. It scrolls away once the user has moved past the end of the table.
- Every column header sorts that table when clicked. The first click sorts ascending. Clicking the same header again sorts descending. The active header shows the direction. Each table sorts on its own. Total and grand total rows stay at the bottom. A table keeps its original order until a header is clicked.
- When a table has a # index column, that column is right-aligned, header and cells.
- A frozen first column (`sticky left-*`) is frozen in the header row too: its header cell stays above the header cells that scroll under it and above the frozen body cells, through both sideways and vertical scrolling. `app/globals.css` does this for every `thead th.sticky` with a `left-*` class, so new tables with a frozen column get it automatically.
- Keep each report table in a `report-table` wrapper so wide tables can scroll sideways. Pin the header from the page scroll while the table crosses the title and filters. Do not use CSS `top` sticky for that pin: horizontal scrolling makes the table its own scroll container, and the header then sticks inside the table. Do not put `overflow: hidden` on a table wrapper.
- Sideways scrolling in a report table stops firmly at the table's edges, with no rubber-band bounce (`overscroll-behavior-x: none` on `.report-table`).

### Charts

Below `md`, line and bar charts draw on a 340-unit-wide canvas instead of 640, so axis labels render at about 11–12px and the chart fills its height. Month labels show every other month when there are more than 7. Desktop keeps the 640-unit canvas.

### Cap progress

A cap progress bar, the percent beside the goal, and Left to cap use the share of the goal. Past 100% is red. 100% is green. From 90% up to 100% is orange. From 70% up to 90% is yellow. Below 70% is blue. Every cap report uses this scale.

## Owner Reports

The reports menu button shows only the current report’s number. Hovering it opens the full menu. A click outside that menu closes it, does not activate whatever is underneath, and the button returns to the number.

Owner Summary’s production table uses the same periods as the timeline charts. A range of 62 days or shorter has one row per day, and the section title reads Day-by-Day Production. A longer range has one row per month, and the title reads Month-over-Month Production. The first column says Day or Month to match. Clicking a row keeps the scorecards on that period, compared with the same period last year. When the date range is This month, Last month, This quarter, Last quarter, or a custom range no longer than four months, clicking that title opens Day-by-Day, Week-by-Week, and Month-over-Month. Week-by-Week groups Sunday through Saturday, keeps a partial week at either end of the range, and labels each week like Jul 01 - Jul 04, with the month on both dates. It compares a week with the same calendar dates one year earlier. The first column says Week while that choice is on. Choosing a granularity the range no longer allows returns the table to the automatic day or month rows. An up arrow on the right of that title hides the scorecards so the table moves up. A down arrow brings them back. That arrow uses the same dark background and hover as All Agents, and it slides in from the right of the title with the same brief bright wash. The production table has a max height matching the screen below the filters. If the data in the table is not enough to fill that height, the Total row sits right after the last row with no blank space. When the rows exceed that height, the Total row stays pinned at the bottom while the rows scroll. While the user scrolls, once that section title reaches the filters it stays there with the table header, and the table header keeps its first column. Scrolling up from the first row returns to the scorecards. When the scorecards are hidden, that same block moves up below the filters. Listings Closed and Buyers Closed column counts only include records where transaction_status is Closed. Clicking any metric number in the production table (excluding Month/Date, Operating Expenses, and Closed Net Income) collapses the table to show just that row, hides the scorecards, and displays a transaction breakdown table below listing all records that make up that clicked figure, with a pinned total row and an All Months (or All Days / All Weeks) back button to restore the full table. The Month/Date column is the only column that updates the scorecards; clicking it hides the transactions table if visible, restores the All Months (or All Days / All Weeks) view, and updates the scorecards for that period.

Leaderboard by Date ranks closed and pending transactions. The last words of the title choose whether the report range applies to `closing_date` or `acceptance_date`. `closing_date` is the default. Those words, and the date filter label, use the field label saved for that key in the transaction schema. A team that names `acceptance_date` Pending Date sees Pending Date in the title and Pending Date Range on the filter. The same rule applies to `closing_date`. Its metric lists every enabled transaction field whose control type is a dropdown (Select Dropdown) or a list picker (Agent Picker), except `gci_type`, `transaction_status`, and `finance_status`. The selected field is what each row groups by. Sales volume and units stay the two boards. Lead Source is the default metric.

Agent Earnings and Entity Payments are extra metric choices. Each payment record is joined to its transaction, so the report keeps that transaction’s status and the selected date. Archived payments stay out. The left board sums the payment amount received by each agent or entity. The right board counts those payment records. While one of those metrics is selected, an Agent or Entity dropdown lists the payees in the current view. Unchecking a name removes that payee from both boards. Its counts are payment records after the other filters. Every metric except Entity Payments has an exclude team leads checkbox, labeled Exclude Team Lead when the client has exactly one team lead and Exclude Team Leads otherwise. Checked, it removes transactions whose primary agent has `is_team_lead` true from both boards and from the filters. Agent Earnings instead removes those agents as payees from the Agent dropdown and both boards. Entity Payments does not show the checkbox.

The metric is the first words of the report title. The selected date field’s label is the last words. Both use the title font, a different color, and no border or background. Clicking either opens its list. The date filter on this report is labeled with that same field label plus Range.

The % Δ column header has an info note. Hover or click it to read that the column is how much each value of the selected metric changed from the same dates in the prior year, and that a dash means the prior year was zero.

Pending scorecards reserve the same space as the Total and Closed comparison row, so the name and amount sit on the same line. The Total label is white in dark mode.

Agent Earnings lists closed and pending transactions by closing date. An Exclude Team Leads checkbox starts unchecked. Checked, it removes those agents from the Agent dropdown, the scorecards, the pivot, and the transaction table. An Earnings Type dropdown sits immediately to the right of the Group filter. Net Commission Income is first and starts as the only checked option. It is the agent payment amounts this report already shows. The other options are commission entities whose type is Equity or Income. An entity is listed when at least one post-split to it remains after the other filters. Its count is those transactions. Checking an entity adds, for each agent, the post-split dollars allocated to that entity. Reset restores Net Commission Income alone. An Agent dropdown lists the payees in the current view. Unchecking a name removes that payee from the scorecards, the pivot, and the transaction table. Its counts are payment records after the other filters. Total, Closed, and Pending scorecards sit above the pivot and sum those earnings. Total and Closed show the change from the same dates last year. Pending reserves that comparison space. Clicking a scorecard keeps the current chart or table in place and filters the visible tables and charts to that status. The selected scorecard’s border matches that scorecard’s amount color. Clicking it again restores every status. The scorecard amounts stay the full totals. While the line chart is open for one agent, those scorecards show that agent’s earnings, and Total and Closed compare them with that agent’s same dates last year. When the line chart is open, those scorecards shift left and a single-line pill inside that card reads Entire period (Year to date), using that date range’s name. If a month or day is selected, a second Total, Closed, and Pending set appears under them, with that month or day in the same kind of pill, and sums that period for the same agent, or for every agent when the selection has no agent. Total and Closed on that set compare the period with the same period last year. Pending reserves that comparison space. Both sets keep the full totals when a status is selected. The line chart sits to the right of the scorecards. Each set, with its period label, sits in its own card. The top card lines up with the line chart’s top edge and the bottom card lines up with the line chart’s bottom edge. The transaction table stays below them. A section title above them reads Agent Earnings and spans the page. While one agent is open, that title reads the agent's name. An up arrow on the right of that title hides the scorecards and the line chart so the table grows upward. A down arrow brings them back. That arrow uses the same dark background and hover as All Agents, and it slides in from the right of the title with the same brief bright wash. The scorecards are centered and keep the same width and the same space between them as the Team Performance scorecards. Total keeps the plain amount color, Closed uses emerald for its name and amount, and Pending uses that same emerald, lighter in light mode and darker in dark mode, for its name and amount. The Total label is white in dark mode. Each cell is the sum of agent payment amounts on those transactions, the same dollars as the Agent Earnings metric on Leaderboard by Date. Each amount has a thin bright green bar with round edges directly under it. The bar’s right end lines up with the number, and its length runs to the left. The longest bar in the agent month cells is the largest of those amounts, and every other bar in that area is its amount divided by that largest amount. The agent total column uses the largest agent total as its own full bar. The month totals use the largest month total as their own full bar. The grand total bar fills its cell. Columns are the months in the selected range, or days when the range is 62 days or shorter. Rows are the agents who received a payment, sorted by the Total column with the highest first. A # column on the left shows each agent’s place in that order, and it stays with the agent when another column is sorted. The transaction table starts sorted by Closing Date, earliest first, and the Closing date header shows that direction. It has a # column to the left of Agent for that order, and that number stays with the row when another column is sorted. Its total row stays pinned to the bottom of the table while the rows scroll. The far-right column is that agent’s total. It has a light left edge and a slightly different background so it reads as the total. A total row stays pinned to the bottom of the pivot while the agent rows scroll. A dash means that agent received nothing in that period. Clicking an amount, a row total, a column total, or the grand total replaces the pivot with a line chart and shows the transaction table with the payments inside that figure and the amount that agent received on each transaction. The line chart’s horizontal axis is the timeline and its vertical axis is those earnings. The current period is an emerald line with a light fill down to the axis. The previous year is a grey line, the same grey as Team Performance. A centered legend reads Current period. Average: amount and Previous year. Average: amount, with the amount standing out. A horizontal line of long dashes in that series color marks each average. A dot marks the selected period. Hovering a period selects the period under the pointer, on the plotted month positions, and shows Agent Earnings, the abbreviated date for this period and the prior year with each amount on the right, and the difference with its percent. When the line chart appears, the selected period’s popup fades in, stays for 3 seconds, and fades away. Clicking another period shows that period’s popup the same way. Hovering a period shows that period’s popup while the pointer is on it. Clicking a period shows that period’s transaction breakdown and moves the dot. Clicking the selected period again clears that period and keeps the chart open. When an agent is selected, the scorecards and the transaction table stay on that agent and show the entire period, with no period dot. When no agent is selected, that click shows the entire period for every agent. A row total or the grand total starts with no period dot. All Agents returns to the pivot. Until an amount is clicked, the transaction table stays hidden. The caption reads like Robert Ito's earnings: August 2026. A month uses its full name and year. A day uses the full date and year. A total uses the Closing Date Range name and dates, such as Year to date (01/01/2026 - 10/11/2026). An All Agents button with an arrow forms the left portion of the Agent Earnings title. When it appears, it slides in from the left of the title with a brief bright wash, then keeps its place. Hovering it turns the button light emerald with dark emerald text so the colors stand out, the pointer is a hand, and clicking it restores every agent. The pivot, while it shows every agent, and the transaction table each stay within the screen height and scroll inside that height. The title, filters, section heading, scorecards, chart, and table header stay fixed. Scrolling the page margins scrolls the rows and leaves that block in place. Both tables use the Owner Summary month table’s dark headers, and their rows alternate, and in light mode the even rows use a slightly darker grey. The Owner Summary month table and both Agent Earnings tables have no horizontal lines between rows.

Team Performance ranks closed and pending transactions. An Exclude Team Leads checkbox starts unchecked. Checked, it removes transactions whose primary agent has `is_team_lead` true from the scorecards, charts, and the other filters. The last words of the title choose whether the report range applies to `closing_date` or `acceptance_date`. `closing_date` is the default. Those words, and the date filter label, use the field label saved for that key, the same way Leaderboard by Date does. Five panels show Sales volume (sum of sales price), Gross commission income (sum of GCI), Units (transaction count), Gross commission percentage (GCI divided by sales price), and Sales price (sales price divided by units) below Units. A sixth panel, Team Commission, sits beside Units. It is a line chart of the sum of `final_amount` on each transaction’s Agent Split commission lines. `final_amount` is the dollar the waterfall shows for that line. An info note on the heading says Team Commission is the commission left after off-the-top deductions and pre-splits are subtracted from Total Commission. Sales price and Gross commission percentage sit together on the bottom row. Those two panels count only transactions whose side is Buyer or Seller. When the Side filter leaves both of those sides unchecked, the scorecards and charts are replaced by a note that the two sections are specific to Buyer and Seller transactions only. Each line is the monthly value against the same months last year, using the selected date. The line is a smooth curve, and it rounds where it levels off. This period has a light fill down to the axis that fades from the line color to transparent at the bottom. The current period line has a light shine along its peaks. A Cumulative checkbox in the filters card, beside Exclude Team Leads, starts unchecked and makes the line charts a running total through the period. The bar charts stay monthly averages. Gross commission percentage and Sales price are vertical bar charts with rounded tops. The current period is a filled bar. Each month has two bars side by side. The current period bar is filled in the chart color. The previous year bar is filled grey. The bars are wide. The previous year line and bar are grey. In light mode that grey is twice as dark as the previous light grey. Legends sit centered under every line chart and bar chart and read Current period. Average: amount and Previous year. Average: amount, with the amount standing out. A horizontal line of long dashes in that series color marks each of those averages on every line chart and bar chart. Hovering a month shows the metric name, the abbreviated date for this period and the prior year with each amount on the right, and the difference with its percent. On the bar charts that month is the pair under the pointer, including the right half of the previous year bar and the left half of the current period bar. The popup stays wide enough for those four lines. Total and Closed show the change from last year. Clicking Total, Closed, or Pending highlights that status on every panel and filters the charts to it. Clicking the selected status again restores the total. The scorecard amounts stay the full totals. Total keeps the plain amount color, Closed uses the section color for its name and amount, and Pending uses that same section color, lighter in light mode and darker in dark mode, for its name and amount. The Total label is white in dark mode.

Caps lists every agent on the selected cap. The cap choice is the report title. It uses the title font, emerald, and no border or background. Clicking it opens a single-choice list with two section headers, Team Caps and Agent Caps. Those headers are not choices. Team caps are listed under the first header and agent caps under the second. A header is left out when that group has no caps in the current list. One cap is open at a time. It defaults to the first listed cap. Reset restores that cap. A cap is listed when at least one agent has that cap’s amount or, for a team cap, its split field, or a contribution on a transaction that passes the other filters. Its count is contributing transactions after the other filters, not after the cap filter. A cap amount of zero does not count as a goal. An agent whose agent-cap amount is zero is left off that cap. An agent whose amount for the selected cap is blank is left off that cap, its total row, and its counts. Status defaults to Closed and Pending, and those are the only statuses. The shared date range and the metric dropdown stay hidden. Cap year is Current or Previous. Current measures from today. Previous measures from the same day one year earlier. Each agent’s cap year starts on the reset date stored in that cap’s date field, so caps with different date fields, and agents with different reset dates, keep their own start and end. Closed and Pending are the contribution dollars in that year. A team cap’s contribution is the team-cap amount on the transaction. An agent cap’s contribution is the post-split dollars paid to the commission entity that cap is mapped to. Contribution is those two added together. Goal is the agent’s cap amount, with the percent of that goal beside a bar. The bar, that percent, and Left to cap use the cap progress colors. Left to cap is the goal minus closed and pending, and it is negative once the agent is past the goal. When an agent cap hides over-allocations, the shown closed, pending, contribution, cumulative, and left to cap stop at the goal, so the bar stays at 100%. The report number, title, subtitle, filters, green section pill, and the line under that pill stay fixed at the top while the table scrolls. The table header stays fixed just below them. Scrolling the page margins scrolls the rows and leaves that block in place. An info icon on the right edge of the green section pill gives the as-of date, says the figures are closed and pending contributions in that year, and says clicking an agent lists the transactions. On the previous cap year that note says the year is measured from the earlier day. The team cap recipient is left out unless that person has a contribution. Rows start in contribution order, highest first. A # column shows that place and stays with the agent when another column is sorted. The total row stays pinned to the bottom of the table while the agent rows scroll, the same way the Agent Earnings total does. Clicking an agent replaces that cap’s table with the transactions in the agent’s cap year: status, primary agent, side, property, closing date, sales price, lead source, contribution, a running cumulative, the same goal bar against the cumulative amount, and Left to cap on the far right. Left to cap is the goal minus that running cumulative, using the same colors as the standings. An All agents button returns to the standings. A Show all paid transactions checkbox in the filters starts unchecked. Checked, that transaction list also shows every transaction in the agent’s cap year that passes the other filters and on which the agent received a payment but nothing went to this cap. Those rows are grey, sit in closing-date order with the others, show a dash for contribution, and carry the cumulative, goal bar, and Left to cap from the row before. They do not change the standings, the total row, or the cap counts.

